=== CELL 2a: DOCUMENT-SIDE SIGNAL — what is there to survive into? ===
  the mechanism claim, measured against all 10,166 ads directly

  family      side   what the query asks for               ads   share
  language    a      ads DEMANDING Norwegian              5703 56.10%
  language    b      ads stating Norwegian NOT required     93  0.91%
  occupation  a      Jeg er sykepleier.                   1391 13.68%
  occupation  b      Jeg er tømrer.                        166  1.63%
  place       a      Jeg ser etter jobb i Oslo.           1174 11.55%
  place       b      Jeg ser etter jobb i Bergen.          436  4.29%

  THE ASYMMETRY: 5703 ads say they demand Norwegian; 93 say it is not required.
  A negated query has 1.6% as much to land on as its positive twin.

  BUT THE COUNT ALONE DOES NOT CARRY THE HYPOTHESIS, and saying it did would be wrong.
  The B-sides are comparable in size: language 93, occupation 166, place 436.
  Language is only 1.8x rarer than `tømrer`, not orders of magnitude.
  So if recovery differs sharply, scarcity is not the whole mechanism — the KIND of
  signal matters too: an ABSENCE stated in an ad is itself a negation, and dilutes
  on the document side exactly as it does on the query side.

  METHOD-MATCHED, because the comparison above is not like-for-like. Occupation and
  place are KEYWORD hits in the embedded text; language is an LLM judgment. Counting
  language the same way occupation is counted — a requirement negated within 90 chars
  of a mention of the Norwegian language:
    ads stating the absence IN WORDS: 334 (3.29%)
    vs `tømrer` 166 (1.63%) and `Bergen` 436 (4.29%)
  Lexically the absence is NOT rarer than `tømrer` (334 vs 166). Document-side
  SCARCITY is therefore not the mechanism: enough ads say it in words. If recovery is
  still 0, what fails is the ENCODER's handling of those words, not their absence.

