=== VERDICT: THE HYPOTHESIS SPLITS ===

  CONFIRMED — the consequence IS asymmetric.
    Equal query-side dilution, unequal retrieval outcome. Content queries pull their
    own signal to 7.1x and 3.9x the base rate. The negated language query reaches
    1.1x — chance.
    Ranking overlap agrees in direction, but only 1 of 4 cosine bins clear the
    power guard, so that arm is suggestive and not decisive.

  DISCONFIRMED — the REASON cell 1b gave for it was wrong.
    Cell 1b said the negation has 'nothing to survive into: almost no advertisement
    states the ABSENCE of a requirement'. Measured the same way `tømrer` is measured,
    334 ads state it in words — MORE than the 166 that say `tømrer`.
    SCARCITY IS NOT THE MECHANISM. The document-side signal is there, in comparable
    quantity, and retrieval still does not use it.

  THE CORRECTED MECHANISM, which is stronger for the architecture argument, not weaker.
    The document-side statement of an absence is ITSELF a negation — `norsk er ikke et
    krav`. Mean pooling dilutes it on the document side exactly as it dilutes the query.
    Two averaged-away negations cannot find each other in a metric space. So the failure
    cannot be fixed by more coverage or more data: the data is ALREADY THERE and unusable.
    Only extracting the constraint into a typed predicate can reach it.

  WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and must not be quoted as settled.
    - The 334 lexical hits are an unvalidated keyword floor; the census labelled only
      93 ads `explicitly_not_required`. Either the pattern over-fires or the census
      under-fires, and which one is unmeasured.
    - Five pairs, one encoder, dense channel only. No confidence intervals.
    - The cosine-bin arm needs more sweep points per bin before it can carry weight.
